12. OUTLIER DETECTION USING THE IQR METHOD
---------------------------------------------------------------
# Choose one numeric column to inspect (replace "Age")
col = "Age"

Q1 = df[col].quantile(0.25)
Q3 = df[col].quantile(0.75)
IQR = Q3 - Q1

lower_bound = Q1 - 1.5 * IQR
upper_bound = Q3 + 1.5 * IQR

outliers = df[(df[col] < lower_bound) | (df[col] > upper_bound)]
print("Number of outliers:", len(outliers))
print(outliers[[col]].head())

# Visual check with a boxplot
sns.boxplot(x=df[col])
plt.show()

---------------------------------------------------------------
13. OUTLIER TREATMENT: CAPPING (OPTIONAL)
---------------------------------------------------------------
Capping limits extreme values to the IQR boundaries. Use only when the values
are not valid observations you need to preserve.

# Example for quick EDA / data cleaning (replace "Age")
df["Age"] = df["Age"].clip(lower_bound, upper_bound)

# For machine learning, calculate bounds from TRAINING DATA ONLY.
# Example for a single numeric column:
col = "Age"
Q1_train = X_train[col].quantile(0.25)
Q3_train = X_train[col].quantile(0.75)
IQR_train = Q3_train - Q1_train
lower_train = Q1_train - 1.5 * IQR_train
upper_train = Q3_train + 1.5 * IQR_train

X_train[col] = X_train[col].clip(lower_train, upper_train)
X_test[col] = X_test[col].clip(lower_train, upper_train)

# Important: do not run the example above unless "Age" is a numeric feature
# present in BOTH X_train and X_test. Repeat for other selected columns as needed.

OTHER OPTIONS:
- Correct an outlier if it is a data-entry error and you know the true value.
- Remove a row only when it is erroneous or outside the scope of the analysis.
- Keep genuine extreme values when they are meaningful.
- Do not automatically cap every numeric column; use domain knowledge and plots.

